<p>Prices and availability can shift during Prime Day, so check the live listing on Amazon before purchasing. The Paperwhite deal at under $100 is particularly notable — it is the lowest price for a Kindle with warm front light and waterproofing.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="why-upgrade-this-prime-day">Why Upgrade Your Kindle This Prime Day</h2>

<p>If you own an older Kindle (pre-2021 models), there are several reasons to upgrade during this Prime Day window:</p>

<h3>Better EPUB Support</h3>
<p>Starting in 2024, Amazon began accepting EPUB files as the standard upload format via Send to Kindle. Modern Kindles handle EPUB natively — you can email EPUB files directly to your device, and Amazon converts them server-side. Older Kindles still rely on the older MOBI pipeline, which is being phased out.</p>

<h3>Color E Ink for Comics and Illustrations</h3>
<p>The Kindle Colorsoft and the upcoming Kindle XL offer color E Ink displays. If you read comics, graphic novels, or illustrated EPUBs, color is a genuine upgrade — covers render in full color, diagrams are readable, and the text remains sharp at 300 PPI.</p>

<h3>New AI Features</h3>
<p>Amazon is rolling out <strong>Kindle Recaps</strong>, <strong>Story So Far</strong>, and <strong>Ask This Book</strong> to newer Kindle models. These AI-powered features work best on current-generation hardware. If you want to use "Ask This Book" to search inside your sideloaded EPUBs, you will need a 2025 or newer Kindle.</p>

<h3>Faster Page Turns and Smoother Navigation</h3>
<p>The 12th-generation Paperwhite and newer models have noticeably faster processors. Page turns are instant, the store navigation is smoother, and Kindle Web Browser is actually usable.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="transfer-library-to-new-kindle">How to Transfer Your Library to a New Kindle</h2>

<p>The good news: transferring your Amazon-purchased books to a new Kindle is almost automatic. The tricky part is moving <strong>non-Amazon EPUBs</strong> — books you downloaded from other stores, free public domain titles from Project Gutenberg, or EPUB files you created yourself.</p>

<h3>Amazon-Purchased Books (Automatic)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Sign in to your Amazon account on the new Kindle</li>
  <li>Go to your <strong>Library</strong> from the home screen</li>
  <li>Tap <strong>All Items</strong> (not just Downloaded)</li>
  <li>Tap any book to download it to the new device</li>
  <li>Your reading progress, highlights, and notes sync automatically via Whispersync</li>
</ol>
<p>If you have hundreds of books, hold and tap to select multiple, then tap <strong>Download All</strong>.</p>

<h3>Sideloaded EPUBs (Manual Transfer)</h3>
<p>For EPUB files you have collected outside of Amazon, you have three transfer methods:</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Method</th>
  <th>Requires</th>
  <th>Best For</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><strong>Send to Kindle</strong></td>
  <td>Wi-Fi, Amazon account</td>
  <td>Single files, occasional transfers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><strong>USB Cable</strong></td>
  <td>USB-C cable, computer</td>
  <td>Batch transfers, large libraries</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><strong>Calibre</strong></td>
  <td>Calibre installed, USB cable</td>
  <td>Full library management, metadata editing</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h4>Method 1: Send to Kindle (Easiest)</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Go to <strong>Amazon.com &gt; Account &gt; Content &amp; Devices &gt; Preferences &gt; Personal Document Settings</strong></li>
  <li>Find your <strong>Send-to-Kindle Email</strong> (e.g., <code><a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="0a73657f78646b676f4a6163646e666f24696567">[email&#160;protected]</a></code>)</li>
  <li>Attach the EPUB file to an email and send it to that address</li>
  <li>Within a minute, the book appears on your Kindle</li>
</ol>
<p>This method works for EPUB files up to 50 MB and preserves bookmarks and notes. It does require Wi-Fi on the Kindle.</p>

<h4>Method 2: USB Cable (Best for Batch Transfers)</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Connect your Kindle to your computer via USB-C</li>
  <li>The Kindle appears as an external drive (e.g., <strong>KINDLE</strong> or <strong>Internal Storage</strong>)</li>
  <li>Open the <strong>documents</strong> folder on the Kindle drive</li>
  <li>Drag and drop your EPUB files into the <strong>documents</strong> folder</li>
  <li>Safely eject the Kindle — the books appear in your Library</li>
</ol>
<p>USB transfer is fast and works offline. It is the best method for moving 50+ EPUBs at once.</p>

<h4>Method 3: Calibre (Best for Library Management)</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Add your EPUBs to Calibre's library via <strong>Add Books</strong></li>
  <li>Edit metadata (titles, covers, series, tags) if needed</li>
  <li>Connect your Kindle via USB</li>
  <li>Select books and click <strong>Send to Device</strong></li>
  <li>Calibre auto-converts to the optimal format for your Kindle model</li>
</ol>
<p>If Calibre is new to you, see our <a href="/blog/calibre-cli-batch-conversion/">Calibre CLI batch conversion guide</a> for the command-line version of this workflow.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="convert-epub-for-kindle">Converting EPUBs for Your New Kindle</h2>

<p>Most EPUB files work fine on Kindle out of the box, but some formatting issues can arise — especially with complex layouts, embedded fonts, or fixed-layout EPUBs.</p>

<h3>Common Issues and Fixes</h3>

<table>
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>Issue</th>
  <th>Cause</th>
  <th>Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td>Missing cover</td>
  <td>EPUB cover not recognized by Amazon's converter</td>
  <td>Re-embed cover as the first image in the EPUB</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Font too small</td>
  <td>CSS specifies a fixed font-size</td>
  <td>Remove absolute font-size declarations from CSS</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Table of contents missing</td>
  <td>No <code>nav.xhtml</code> in the EPUB</td>
  <td>Generate an NCX or nav file in Calibre</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Images not showing</td>
  <td>Image paths are relative to the wrong directory</td>
  <td>Rebundle EPUB with correct <code>href</code> paths</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Paragraphs not justified</td>
  <td>Missing <code>text-align: justify</code> in publisher CSS</td>
  <td>Use Calibre to add justification in conversion settings</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>For a deeper look at optimizing EPUBs specifically for Kindle hardware, see our <a href="/blog/epub-to-kindle-formatting-2026/">EPUB to Kindle formatting guide</a>.</p>

<h3>Should You Convert EPUB to AZW3 or KFX?</h3>
<p>Modern Kindles (2021 and later) support EPUB natively via the Send to Kindle pipeline. You do not need to convert EPUB to AZW3 or KFX unless you want specific Kindle features:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Leave as EPUB</strong> — works fine for standard reading, the Kindle converts it server-side</li>
  <li><strong>Convert to AZW3</strong> — gives you more consistent font rendering and better justification on older Kindles</li>
  <li><strong>Convert to KFX</strong> — unlocks enhanced typography, page flip, and panel view on newer Kindles</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are using Calibre to manage your library, you can configure it to auto-convert to AZW3 or KFX during the "Send to Device" step. The default EPUB is fine for most readers.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="back-up-before-trade-in">Backing Up Your Books Before Trade-In</h2>

<p>Amazon offers a <strong>Kindle Trade-In</strong> program that gives you up to 20% off a new Kindle plus a gift card for your old device. If you plan to trade in your old Kindle, make sure to back up your content first:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Download DRM-free EPUBs</strong> from Amazon — go to <strong>Manage Your Content and Devices</strong>, find each book, and select <strong>Download EPUB</strong> (only available for books where the publisher opted into DRM-free delivery)</li>
  <li><strong>Copy sideloaded files</strong> — connect your old Kindle via USB and copy any EPUBs, PDFs, or documents from the <strong>documents</strong> folder to your computer</li>
  <li><strong>Export notes and highlights</strong> — on a Kindle, you can export your highlights via the <strong>Your Highlights</strong> page at <code>read.amazon.com/notebook</code></li>
  <li><strong>Factory reset</strong> your old Kindle after verifying everything is transferred to the new device</li>
</ol>

<p>For a detailed walkthrough of backing up your Amazon library, see <a href="/blog/amazon-drm-free-epub-download-2026/">Amazon DRM-Free EPUB 2026: How to Download Kindle Books as EPUB</a>. If you are migrating from a very old Kindle, our <a href="/blog/old-kindle-bricked-migrate-epub-2026/">guide to rescuing books from bricked Kindles</a> covers extraction methods for legacy devices.</p>

